blem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+12x+13.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+12x+13) \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}+24x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+24x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+8x+4 & =0 \\\\ (3x+2)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3},\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=-2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $x=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-99019/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99019"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344544/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "919dc54f-5cc5-52ba-abe2-ef8a234b0ec9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344544/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a6531eb-b67a-5542-9e05-a69455577972" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344544/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-344544/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344544/field-2/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "346db744-fd1a-56a4-9fb3-c6a9538fd256" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344544/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "61091b4e-5f84-58d2-b183-e89cbfab11ba" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344544/field-2/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-344544/field-2/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344544" :question/id #uuid "1f148c92-f658-549e-a0cb-5ac12616def8" :question/math-academy-id "q-344544" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+24x-11$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+24x-11) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+18x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+18x+24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-4$ and $x=-2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-4$ and $x=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344544/field-1" "q-344544/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344544"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-131273/selection/1a3026bbdb61d32e9cfe987b50907ca567cce1ecc4e6bb9b82a45a3012b69a4e" :answer/id #uuid "e914e1f3-57bd-5e74-a152-921dea275d3c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\pm 1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131273/selection/9f75d63bbce36d71b1687120236f1905d3381f1a10419d07fe4d39d3b5a3b550" :answer/id #uuid "55d176e1-643e-54db-96c0-f0a1d17deb29" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131273/selection/fc01e83cbc0a91221270553ac2afbd8c6a7c87ced1ce0529b4dbc53f926f7026" :answer/id #uuid "9e6648cf-52ab-5eff-aa0f-8735884d2f05" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131273/selection/157d972daa5d9f68091f886eeb88f78e05562b949cefc210c01145d2b125060a" :answer/id #uuid "dcf2f269-1631-5ce9-b6d1-5f62a9e1cd57" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131273/selection/63d45c0aa1ee41d1a647ec44aa152173aa975d3f3697b59fd5b3ba50982e0865" :answer/id #uuid "5aeee68d-01ca-5caa-8229-babe4a18c414" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131273/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6eaa94aa-f031-5e66-9fc2-68eee4c02c08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-131273/selection/1a3026bbdb61d32e9cfe987b50907ca567cce1ecc4e6bb9b82a45a3012b69a4e" :answer-field/choices ["q-131273/selection/1a3026bbdb61d32e9cfe987b50907ca567cce1ecc4e6bb9b82a45a3012b69a4e" "q-131273/selection/9f75d63bbce36d71b1687120236f1905d3381f1a10419d07fe4d39d3b5a3b550" "q-131273/selection/fc01e83cbc0a91221270553ac2afbd8c6a7c87ced1ce0529b4dbc53f926f7026" "q-131273/selection/157d972daa5d9f68091f886eeb88f78e05562b949cefc210c01145d2b125060a" "q-131273/selection/63d45c0aa1ee41d1a647ec44aa152173aa975d3f3697b59fd5b3ba50982e0865"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-131273" :question/id #uuid "42598c74-a5de-588f-a397-c6d324d9491a" :question/math-academy-id "q-131273" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function  $f(x)=({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){e}^{4{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){e}^{4{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4})}^{′}{e}^{4{x}^{2}}+({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){({e}^{4{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{4{x}^{2}}+(8x)({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){e}^{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =[2x+8{x}^{3}-10x]{e}^{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(8{x}^{3}-8x){e}^{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{x}^{3}-8x){e}^{4{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{4{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{4{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-8x & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 8x(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,-1,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-131273/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-131273"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344857/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "6365089c-ca2d-5190-84f6-7bb5774db6c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344857/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6bc40150-6359-5379-bf14-bc3763ee9fd9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344857/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-344857/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344857/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "9f8aad47-7111-501c-a0e7-3e02ef73e2c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344857/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd796db4-1258-57be-90fe-ae8a87711227" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344857/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-344857/field-2/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344857" :question/id #uuid "8ed5c2a5-9f71-5933-881b-13c938f0f660" :question/math-academy-id "q-344857" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function $f(x)=(x+1)^{3}{e}^{x-1}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+1)^{3}{e}^{x-1}) \\\\  & ={((x+1)^{3})}^{′}{e}^{x-1}+(x+1)^{3}{({e}^{x-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =3(x+1)^{2}{e}^{x-1}+(x+1)^{3}{e}^{x-1} \\\\  & =[3(x+1)^{2}+(x+1)^{3}]{e}^{x-1} \\\\  & =(x+1)^{2}(x+4){e}^{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}(x+4){e}^{x-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x-1}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{x-1}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}(x+4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-4$ and $x=-1.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-344857/field-1" "q-344857/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344857"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344841/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "05240ba4-db34-576a-90ed-c51e45ea9162" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344841/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "12770882-c5bc-5e68-9a98-68df38b775ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344841/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "932d3d2a-75ab-5536-850a-f820d7653d7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344841/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-344841/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-344841/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344841/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "30c619e9-cd92-5e27-aad9-c6625c36ee9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344841/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd5c5fc8-79b7-5493-a1d2-f5bab2ec1c57" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344841/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-344841/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344841" :question/id #uuid "d80a50a6-7416-5124-9e38-1f3238eca785" :question/math-academy-id "q-344841" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2})}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-12x){e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-12x){e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-12x & =0 \\\\ 3x(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are  $x=0$ and  $x=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344841/field-1" "q-344841/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344841"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-131185/selection/39f508abc01e725e09148a85c8f1573b302895828a712352740012f54d43f896" :answer/id #uuid "14de8f15-42e5-5e5a-a8ba-790e42857927" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131185/selection/b827a1a3de8604ffbd58f7d5f08f86ec64dcb67f95f6b7f081772661830a386a" :answer/id #uuid "a60f2d6f-75f8-5f68-b8e5-a56ab18e90dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131185/selection/54ac610d4774fee0ede6478b691a6eacc7c927529e978f51a18e757cb18525eb" :answer/id #uuid "69e43028-d7a3-576b-8b1b-96256c8d3cc9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131185/selection/6ecc08a56b39b0c7f4a22cd394d32888fc0ad3f496ca7dfa8af0d1002bbdca0e" :answer/id #uuid "9ec5b48f-0971-518e-82b4-b55e96db9440" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=9$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131185/selection/bca663ed02819cba52acde92bcbf505a77d7fa677edf123124e75edb4a2f6bfd" :answer/id #uuid "5b305ee6-8380-5997-98ae-86cdd4dbca88" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-9$ and $x=9$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131185/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2c31a122-f7a5-5bd4-91ae-ef3037fd761a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-131185/selection/39f508abc01e725e09148a85c8f1573b302895828a712352740012f54d43f896" :answer-field/choices ["q-131185/selection/39f508abc01e725e09148a85c8f1573b302895828a712352740012f54d43f896" "q-131185/selection/b827a1a3de8604ffbd58f7d5f08f86ec64dcb67f95f6b7f081772661830a386a" "q-131185/selection/54ac610d4774fee0ede6478b691a6eacc7c927529e978f51a18e757cb18525eb" "q-131185/selection/6ecc08a56b39b0c7f4a22cd394d32888fc0ad3f496ca7dfa8af0d1002bbdca0e" "q-131185/selection/bca663ed02819cba52acde92bcbf505a77d7fa677edf123124e75edb4a2f6bfd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-131185" :question/id #uuid "78cd88cf-0938-5eca-ad33-9a676a3cd4cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-131185" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}+6x)}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{2}+6x} \\\\  & =(2x+6)\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+6)\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+6x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{2}+6x}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+6)\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+6x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+6 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-3.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical point of $f(x)$ is $x=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-131185/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-131185"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344861/field-1/df4ef3a75f6d71027299791921d9536d50890079e16123ff435f8ed8ac6150b0" :answer/id #uuid "e9e3ec4d-9089-5218-903e-ead0e59b1084" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344861/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7fee5fab-e353-5007-85ff-a4867975b0fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344861/field-1/df4ef3a75f6d71027299791921d9536d50890079e16123ff435f8ed8ac6150b0" :answer-field/choices ["q-344861/field-1/df4ef3a75f6d71027299791921d9536d50890079e16123ff435f8ed8ac6150b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344861/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "7f0ef56e-679b-525c-a933-85b1ae11aeb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344861/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "cbcc8226-2ba9-5f7f-b76c-8c32d3f53b0e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344861/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-344861/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344861/field-3/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d" :answer/id #uuid "0cca3c2e-81ae-516c-8a16-a19e2a8671fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344861/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "642d4508-277d-5b8c-ab1f-a06ed3fc6689" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344861/field-3/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d" :answer-field/choices ["q-344861/field-3/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344861" :question/id #uuid "8e050c4e-a3a3-5fee-80dd-c16191e31da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-344861" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function  $f(x)=({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}-1)}^{′}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+({x}^{2}-1){({e}^{-{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+({x}^{2}-1)(-2x){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}(1-({x}^{2}-1)) \\\\  & =2x(2-{x}^{2}){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2x({x}^{2}-2){e}^{-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x({x}^{2}-2){e}^{-{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x({x}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ -2x(x-\\sqrt{2})(x+\\sqrt{2}) & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\sqrt{2},\\,0,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ $x=0,$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx=-\\sqrt{2},\\,x=0,\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-344861/field-1" "q-344861/field-2" "q-344861/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344861"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99056/selection/1f5e9570249f9e2622f3d5d63abffa3331e89a167568104f9602084907c01516" :answer/id #uuid "ed53dbec-72a9-52d1-aafe-4adde0b83d1c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99056/selection/1288db97e8c08fe1e59253bc39f36416fd21e4daf73730ee4bb5aebee1b12042" :answer/id #uuid "27548e7f-003a-5c17-957d-ead77ed104e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99056/selection/34a90448fe55c75fb547fdd4c1a03702d531839c7375a5f2c3b41cb67f1a5db9" :answer/id #uuid "2f38057c-3792-5bee-b2c7-3e25851fa860" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99056/selection/2cf4e3c0e0a087384915508e30ce9bc4d98bf3544b6118fa15fad1fd62bad5bb" :answer/id #uuid "f5ec8f46-3f19-5428-bd72-e1b30b2308ed" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99056/selection/c362b6d4c423a0c9ae4df7e94fd92f8a651564debb3e0159f8464febde656e74" :answer/id #uuid "57986211-8a2c-5c12-929b-3f3350de3c24" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99056/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f42e289-fda4-50ef-86d2-078bbf460904" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-99056/selection/1f5e9570249f9e2622f3d5d63abffa3331e89a167568104f9602084907c01516" :answer-field/choices ["q-99056/selection/1f5e9570249f9e2622f3d5d63abffa3331e89a167568104f9602084907c01516" "q-99056/selection/1288db97e8c08fe1e59253bc39f36416fd21e4daf73730ee4bb5aebee1b12042" "q-99056/selection/34a90448fe55c75fb547fdd4c1a03702d531839c7375a5f2c3b41cb67f1a5db9" "q-99056/selection/2cf4e3c0e0a087384915508e30ce9bc4d98bf3544b6118fa15fad1fd62bad5bb" "q-99056/selection/c362b6d4c423a0c9ae4df7e94fd92f8a651564debb3e0159f8464febde656e74"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99056" :question/id #uuid "70a98919-dd4e-535e-b918-4ce91d1560b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-99056" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}-2}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)-(2x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)-(2x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4+6x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6x+4}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6x+4}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-99056/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99056"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-48741"] :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+4)-x\\cdot ({x}^{2}+4)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)-x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-99053/selection/5ae5bd5f0ff87d7a7d38712354fc7e51cfcc2c56cec99dca9e7c6a359da02666" :answer/id #uuid "478d8141-35d1-5fcf-89e5-75f83f0e847b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99053/selection/4b283939f44505d43127cbae6d5fbe4324d5fea522550e86d0d23cbb2428a0bb" :answer/id #uuid "05781d01-edac-50e4-8c7e-f6d1a7352445" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99053/selection/caa1480127d61a1777b06d605f1adffc9022d9830425fbcfd75797676392e878" :answer/id #uuid "7c3755f9-801a-5a08-af24-f58c4cd9755e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99053/selection/c3cd4a7a3bfba378b6f3080cf752c033d7a765bf3e86de10fcaeda1183dc6c0b" :answer/id #uuid "7ced6d18-640e-5682-af98-fb3d7c2a0eba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99053/selection/cd6e664c5292cdd6ba95b408ec4c110b6632df90f7cafaf24b63abcb7861923b" :answer/id #uuid "a33f0e13-f1b8-58f5-af37-6f5d4fcddf2e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99053/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3480d180-4a78-572e-b2ac-cf2de69f3f0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-99053/selection/5ae5bd5f0ff87d7a7d38712354fc7e51cfcc2c56cec99dca9e7c6a359da02666" :answer-field/choices ["q-99053/selection/5ae5bd5f0ff87d7a7d38712354fc7e51cfcc2c56cec99dca9e7c6a359da02666" "q-99053/selection/4b283939f44505d43127cbae6d5fbe4324d5fea522550e86d0d23cbb2428a0bb" "q-99053/selection/caa1480127d61a1777b06d605f1adffc9022d9830425fbcfd75797676392e878" "q-99053/selection/c3cd4a7a3bfba378b6f3080cf752c033d7a765bf3e86de10fcaeda1183dc6c0b" "q-99053/selection/cd6e664c5292cdd6ba95b408ec4c110b6632df90f7cafaf24b63abcb7861923b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99053" :question/id #uuid "42dd53a1-4ccb-5086-adb5-70b77ded4e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-99053" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+6}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(2x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(2x+1)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+12-4{x}^{2}-2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-2x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12-2x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 12-2x-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-3$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-3$ and $x=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-99053/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99053"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344867/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "c1b5578c-54d6-518a-a821-205041416eee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344867/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "47cfa3b4-4572-5b09-ba58-7f4870cf5b27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344867/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-344867/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344867/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "01269afc-9ab8-5379-b77a-d6e39aa9dfa2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344867/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "70591605-abc1-5f53-968f-adbeabf56a79" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344867/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-344867/field-2/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344867" :question/id #uuid "5bba6115-49a3-547a-a267-5ba20a9dc0de" :question/math-academy-id "q-344867" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function  $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+8}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)-(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+8)-(x+1)\\cdot (2x)}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+8-(2{x}^{2}+2x)}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}-2x+8}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-2x+8}{({x}^{2}+8)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-2x+8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-4$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So, $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are \n\n$$\nx=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,x=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-344867/field-1" "q-344867/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344867"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-48922/selection/9705668966fb8d1af3eb916ffbf68287f9daac20760c8675176a76fe3a09d29a" :answer/id #uuid "2bb88612-80fa-55c1-93f4-ca16c52bbb56" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48922/selection/1f5a383877aafcae60bcef6d95ad1941c3c517b3c75619f5440aba98e4287549" :answer/id #uuid "e2f8178d-1c28-5856-9c84-fde98681ad8d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48922/selection/b827a1a3de8604ffbd58f7d5f08f86ec64dcb67f95f6b7f081772661830a386a" :answer/id #uuid "5ed924b9-09ab-549d-ba69-254be037cb45" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48922/selection/157d972daa5d9f68091f886eeb88f78e05562b949cefc210c01145d2b125060a" :answer/id #uuid "fd4b81be-cb1e-5fa2-97c7-f8cc896652aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48922/selection/517b13fa7d7411493417798333c215e538be20515774e23158b4c71536604686" :answer/id #uuid "9f601d0e-caf2-5198-962b-b5a5172bffbb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48922/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3bd752b4-ddd6-5a48-8a52-6e03dd3f1a55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-48922/selection/1f5a383877aafcae60bcef6d95ad1941c3c517b3c75619f5440aba98e4287549" :answer-field/choices ["q-48922/selection/9705668966fb8d1af3eb916ffbf68287f9daac20760c8675176a76fe3a09d29a" "q-48922/selection/1f5a383877aafcae60bcef6d95ad1941c3c517b3c75619f5440aba98e4287549" "q-48922/selection/b827a1a3de8604ffbd58f7d5f08f86ec64dcb67f95f6b7f081772661830a386a" "q-48922/selection/157d972daa5d9f68091f886eeb88f78e05562b949cefc210c01145d2b125060a" "q-48922/selection/517b13fa7d7411493417798333c215e538be20515774e23158b4c71536604686"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48922" :question/id #uuid "811d06c8-d944-5b16-a846-13c01b72d3b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-48922" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+3}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+3)-(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+3)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+3)-(x+1)\\cdot (2x)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3-2{x}^{2}-2x}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-2x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3-2x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+3)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 3-2x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-3$ and $x=1.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-3$ and $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-48922/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48922"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-314779/field-1/9f3fe0666fe667480d480b2f3c4f24f5e2ac276a7450efc6201b6fbe6a76b60c" :answer/id #uuid "66e29b20-5353-5798-9c3d-e33a9b89e0e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-314779/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "36769b3f-f06c-5e2c-9870-7522211fbcb7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-314779/field-1/9f3fe0666fe667480d480b2f3c4f24f5e2ac276a7450efc6201b6fbe6a76b60c" :answer-field/choices ["q-314779/field-1/9f3fe0666fe667480d480b2f3c4f24f5e2ac276a7450efc6201b6fbe6a76b60c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-314779/field-2/5cf956242b9863bec1345051ca8ccb3caf8118ef7c2e138f555df8faaf61abd8" :answer/id #uuid "2eb4e0da-df1c-51b1-8a75-f3c9a7ea8475" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-314779/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "a87998e2-01a6-5635-811c-9e1845a1e9b3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-314779/field-2/5cf956242b9863bec1345051ca8ccb3caf8118ef7c2e138f555df8faaf61abd8" :answer-field/choices ["q-314779/field-2/5cf956242b9863bec1345051ca8ccb3caf8118ef7c2e138f555df8faaf61abd8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-314779" :question/id #uuid "6b86cc57-835e-5c4e-92f9-4753683d5914" :question/math-academy-id "q-314779" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ In **ascending order**, the **exact values** of the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$\n\n- setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (0,2\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-314779/field-1" "q-314779/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-314779"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127391/selection/9fd9604a33923dcebe8a17d833e9e1e0665390bc77393075dcdb50de6a12a3a3" :answer/id #uuid "a6d7a4ed-32b8-54d7-9197-4f0546245ca3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127391/selection/dea1585e6aa12673e649629568fe6e0791408665ec0bc01181f248d70e89382e" :answer/id #uuid "109c70b9-d380-527e-8bce-a2dfaa037768" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{21{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127391/selection/bb461af6cbac7e0ebf3d542a60664e443fb7e97f50afaa803007a598cda83fff" :answer/id #uuid "1600cac6-2d8b-56bd-afe7-4f137ba3fcf0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127391/selection/f7591c01da5e93f60c7d3a28ed470ac862989d9bc53f1c0b5579b0bdd5acf72d" :answer/id #uuid "e0ee4789-2daf-59bf-bab5-a5064d6cc2a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127391/selection/7fdb708f0810c1fe78d182b7a6008108a1f44ad8ce9a8e7620ca9a4b7f01c340" :answer/id #uuid "8a716a49-51ec-54e0-ae4e-94352d1c8d25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127391/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4dea5912-59da-54f5-a993-0b791e756393" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127391/selection/9fd9604a33923dcebe8a17d833e9e1e0665390bc77393075dcdb50de6a12a3a3" :answer-field/choices ["q-127391/selection/9fd9604a33923dcebe8a17d833e9e1e0665390bc77393075dcdb50de6a12a3a3" "q-127391/selection/dea1585e6aa12673e649629568fe6e0791408665ec0bc01181f248d70e89382e" "q-127391/selection/bb461af6cbac7e0ebf3d542a60664e443fb7e97f50afaa803007a598cda83fff" "q-127391/selection/f7591c01da5e93f60c7d3a28ed470ac862989d9bc53f1c0b5579b0bdd5acf72d" "q-127391/selection/7fdb708f0810c1fe78d182b7a6008108a1f44ad8ce9a8e7620ca9a4b7f01c340"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127391" :question/id #uuid "3453a5c2-1ed2-5148-a17a-c5359d25adf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-127391" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+n\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0<x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (0,2\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (0,2\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127391/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127391"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-345502/field-1/7b07fa4180fe9201f7944b6f4f5341acec086b4c50de2b4f4928478622402d76" :answer/id #uuid "81fe41b7-4979-5a64-8a1b-70d5ba6404ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345502/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2dbd8c01-b6de-5716-bc3b-e918932d845a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-345502/field-1/7b07fa4180fe9201f7944b6f4f5341acec086b4c50de2b4f4928478622402d76" :answer-field/choices ["q-345502/field-1/7b07fa4180fe9201f7944b6f4f5341acec086b4c50de2b4f4928478622402d76"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345502/field-2/438469ab2d71e1bb8b63f3164bacb1bbb54e29062b14dce6a666b63ac0081609" :answer/id #uuid "7ef4e134-4fb0-5489-b214-2b4a9f035fd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-345502/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "c27c3136-3431-56ab-9dfb-5cb88c60e1c8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-345502/field-2/438469ab2d71e1bb8b63f3164bacb1bbb54e29062b14dce6a666b63ac0081609" :answer-field/choices ["q-345502/field-2/438469ab2d71e1bb8b63f3164bacb1bbb54e29062b14dce6a666b63ac0081609"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-345502" :question/id #uuid "ffec8fbc-60c6-5424-a03e-5c676c82209f" :question/math-academy-id "q-345502" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ In **ascending order**, the **exact values** of the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =arctan\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-345502/field-1" "q-345502/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-345502"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132567/selection/b44290e44641e6fbfb8d9fe45eb9264aadc6d4801e73b4cc89ae8fae0834c012" :answer/id #uuid "5bfcefc6-fe91-5588-84ab-9b06f0fcf4bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132567/selection/74014f0df4913244829447c9cdcb1269005c9cbabedb407a4f565e831942234d" :answer/id #uuid "5b3db0aa-2398-5051-9911-e6c3902fb470" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132567/selection/8d54d5a33a4a6335cbac07172f6ca8a7c8d5747aa3e69924290a2799a513ba90" :answer/id #uuid "80659f67-523a-5486-afad-81d6d4569db2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132567/selection/0bef786c82f91e7cf8c4585a8b2b4252fc10db4bed36e734091535a098004359" :answer/id #uuid "0c96e002-b3b3-52c5-ade0-ddfb4c83c758" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132567/selection/d49d489b8643593ea6da23ce0954b46f7709c930833e7da7a68231c37da9ea83" :answer/id #uuid "7d1f65e1-7ecb-578c-a13b-dd59d0aa9fee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132567/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cc73a482-06bc-563c-93cd-bc92a151934c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-132567/selection/b44290e44641e6fbfb8d9fe45eb9264aadc6d4801e73b4cc89ae8fae0834c012" :answer-field/choices ["q-132567/selection/b44290e44641e6fbfb8d9fe45eb9264aadc6d4801e73b4cc89ae8fae0834c012" "q-132567/selection/74014f0df4913244829447c9cdcb1269005c9cbabedb407a4f565e831942234d" "q-132567/selection/8d54d5a33a4a6335cbac07172f6ca8a7c8d5747aa3e69924290a2799a513ba90" "q-132567/selection/0bef786c82f91e7cf8c4585a8b2b4252fc10db4bed36e734091535a098004359" "q-132567/selection/d49d489b8643593ea6da23ce0954b46f7709c930833e7da7a68231c37da9ea83"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132567" :question/id #uuid "cf3436ad-e1fe-557d-8a36-acddfc3947bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132567" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6}=-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-132567/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132567"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-132569/selection/2ee2cfe615154a44ab76e3add27527ea3aeb13df80b2aae862a301a9198678ea" :answer/id #uuid "0c728fdc-e6f1-5b2d-ba09-1afdb5e76239" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1.02" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132569/selection/db27b2f2bc018a5cfa8cf753b3206b71a0c841d9f937839565741e97af164d8a" :answer/id #uuid "fa97072a-0f11-550a-aed1-36df11609980" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2.39" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132569/selection/0b359bac4fe91aa4470a6d0c34e0267b62a969e2176eab06456a3d6488c80aa6" :answer/id #uuid "7f61b090-6084-58c4-bba2-535c643863ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3.98" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132569/selection/54d14f0033f6e12a90bb55575b6b09c76d171f3913435032d528e066e53e3c3f" :answer/id #uuid "3d92fb77-03c9-59a6-9b42-eda731a3fc62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3.52" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132569/selection/e5f6fe6ca037ea188ee20998b6831b02f3164bd488f349444d2b9512b890fcea" :answer/id #uuid "c835c3a6-4a11-50dc-a36f-f32e59d3c9c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2.78" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-132569/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9cd341b6-4e5e-5216-87f1-95aa03fd0805" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-132569/selection/db27b2f2bc018a5cfa8cf753b3206b71a0c841d9f937839565741e97af164d8a" :answer-field/choices ["q-132569/selection/2ee2cfe615154a44ab76e3add27527ea3aeb13df80b2aae862a301a9198678ea" "q-132569/selection/db27b2f2bc018a5cfa8cf753b3206b71a0c841d9f937839565741e97af164d8a" "q-132569/selection/0b359bac4fe91aa4470a6d0c34e0267b62a969e2176eab06456a3d6488c80aa6" "q-132569/selection/54d14f0033f6e12a90bb55575b6b09c76d171f3913435032d528e066e53e3c3f" "q-132569/selection/e5f6fe6ca037ea188ee20998b6831b02f3164bd488f349444d2b9512b890fcea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-132569" :question/id #uuid "e7337738-5ff0-5479-90bf-c4348b276c02" :question/math-academy-id "q-132569" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{7}{2})+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x≈1.292\\,50$ rounded to five decimal places\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x≈-1.849\\,10$ rounded to five decimal places\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx≈1.292\\,50,\\,x≈-1.849\\,10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x≈-1.849\\,10$ and $x≈1.292\\,50.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}≈-1.849\\,10$ and ${x}_{2}≈1.292\\,50.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈-1.849\\,10\\cdot 1.292\\,50≈-2.39\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-132569/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-132569"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6722"] :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)=2x^3-3x^2+5.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+5) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ 6x(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x=0,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=0$ and $x=1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6723"] :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x) = e^{x^3-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-x}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-x)}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-x} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-1){e}^{{x}^{3}-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-1){e}^{{x}^{3}-x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{x^3-x} \\neq 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by $e^{x^3-x}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-1){e}^{{x}^{3}-x} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}$ and $x=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}$ and $x=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8757"] :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac {x-3}{x^2 -6}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c)=0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-6)-(x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-6)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-6)-(x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6+6x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+6}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+6}{({x}^{2}-6)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+6 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =3 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =3\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=3\\pm \\sqrt 3.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt 6.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=3\\pm \\sqrt 3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9576"] :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = \\sqrt 3\\sin{2x} - \\cos{2x}.$ Find critical points of $f(x)$ for $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c) = 0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡2x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\lt x \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\lt x \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ are \n\n$$\nx =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\qquad x =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in \\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2, \\dfrac\\pi2\\right).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in \\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2, \\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$ are\n\n$$\nx_1 =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\qquad x_2 =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
